<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-total-energy-equation-subsection-bernoulli" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Bernoulli's principle</title>

    <p>
        You may have learned in a physics course about <term>Bernoulli's principle</term>,
        which is a statement of energy conservation for steady, adiabatic flow of an incompressible and <term>inviscid</term> (zero viscosity) fluid.
        For this type of fluid flow,
        the sum of the fluid's kinetic, potential, and pressure energies is constant.
        This usually is expressed using per unit volume quantities as in <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-simple" text="type-global" />.:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-simple" number="yes">
            \frac{\rho V^2}{2} + \rho gz + p = constant
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>V</m> [<m>\text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>] is the flow speed,
        <m>z</m> [m] is the fluid's geometric height,
        <m>p</m> [Pa] is the fluid pressure,
        and <m>\rho</m> [<m>\text{kg} \, \text{m}^{-3}</m>] is the fluid density.
        Dividing <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-simple" text="type-global" /> by <m>\rho</m>
        produces the same statement using specific (per unit mass) quantities:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-simple-specific" number="yes">
            \frac{V^2}{2} + \Phi + p \alpha = constant
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where geopotential <m>\Phi = gz</m> [<m>\text{m}^2 \, \text{s}^{-2}</m>].
    </p>

    <p>
        By applying the steady state (<m>\dfrac{\partial p}{\partial t} = 0</m>),
        adiabatic (<m>\dot{Q} = 0</m>),
        and inviscid (<m>\nu = 0</m>) assumptions to the total energy equation given in <xref ref="eqn-total-energy-equation" text="type-global" />,
        we can derive a related form of Bernoulli's principle:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology" number="yes">
            \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
            + \Phi
            + c_v T
            + p \alpha
            =
            constant
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        The first term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology" text="type-global" />
        corresponds to the first term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-simple-specific" text="type-global" />,
        since both give the kinetic energy of the fluid flow per unit mass.
        The second term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology" text="type-global" />
        corresponds directly to the second term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-simple-specific" text="type-global" />,
        and the fourth term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology" text="type-global" />
        corresponds directly to the third term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-simple-specific" text="type-global" />.
        If the air further is assumed to be incompressible,
        <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology" text="type-global" /> further reduces to
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology-incompressible" number="yes">
            \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
            + \Phi
            + p \alpha
            =
            constant
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology-incompressible" text="type-global" /> says that,
        if the atmosphere is at rest and incompressible,
        an increase in geometric height (equivalent to an increase in geopotential and geopotential height)
        results in a hydrostatic decrease in pressure.
        If the atmosphere is in motion,
        the decrease in pressure will be even larger than hydrostatic!
        Even without an increase in geometric height,
        <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-meteorology-incompressible" text="type-global" />
        says that the faster the air moves,
        the lower its pressure becomes.
    </p>

</subsection>